<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Home on the Range</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written 1872 or 1873 (perhaps as early as <a href="/years/1871">1871</a>); published 1873 or 1874</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In 1871 Dr. Brewster M. Higley moved from Indiana to Smith County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, where he took land under the Homestead Act and lived in a small cabin near West Beaver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>. Moved by the country around him, he wrote the poem &quot;My Western Home&quot;, in 1872 or 1873 by most accounts, though at least one source dates it as early as 1871.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Higley's poem My Western Home appeared in the Smith County Pioneer newspaper and was reprinted in March 1874 in The Kirwin Chief. Daniel E. Kelley (1843–1905), a friend of Higley and a member of the Harlan Brothers Orchestra, worked out a melody for it on his guitar. The original words resemble the modern song but are not the same; the poem did not contain the words on the range. Higley's cabin is listed on the National Register of Historic Places as the Home on the Range Cabin.</p><p>Ranchers, cowboys and other settlers took the song up as a rural anthem, and it spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in various forms; cowboys on the Chisholm Trail seem to have helped carry it through several states. The folklorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lomax">John Lomax</a> printed it in 1910 in Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads, reporting that he had learned it from a black saloon keeper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> who knew it from the trail. In 1925 the Texas composer David W. Guion arranged it as sheet music, published by G. Schirmer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The song's authorship was long disputed. On 26 February 1876 The Kirwin Chief accused The Stockton News of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> a nearly identical poem credited to Mrs. Emma Race of Raceburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>. After Bing Crosby recorded the song with Lennie Hayton and his orchestra for Brunswick Records on 27 September 1933, William and Mary Goodwin sued for copyright infringement in 1934, claiming $500,000; they had published a similar song, An Arizona Home, in 1905. Samuel Moanfeldt, investigating for the Music Publishers Protection Association, found another similar song, Colorado Home, but within a few months concluded that Higley had written the poem and Kelley set it to music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Home on the Range became the state song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> on 30 June 1947 and is often called the unofficial anthem of the American West. A version is one of the seven fight songs of the University of Kansas, played by the Marching Jayhawks at the end of every home athletic event. In 2010 members of the Western Writers of America named it one of the top 100 western songs. Gene Autry, Burl Ives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> and Frank Sinatra were among those who recorded it, and it served as the theme of the 1950s television series The Range Rider.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Home on the Range” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
